<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mauri</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Maurusii</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mauri were the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> people of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, in what is now northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, from whom the names Mauretania and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a> derive. United under kings by the late 3rd century BCE, they became Roman allies and then subjects after their kingdom was annexed in 42 CE. Mauri cavalry served widely in the Roman army.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mauri.jpg" alt="Mauri" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lusius_Quietus_on_Column_of_Trajan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Mauri was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> population of the Kingdom of Mauretania in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>. Strabo, writing in the early 1st century, gives Mauri as the people's own name, adopted into Latin, and Maurusii as the Greek one. The name first meant the Berber population of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, but as the kingdom grew it was also applied to those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, who belonged to the Numidian tribal groups. Both names probably cover the first Berber-speaking peoples; the oldest Libyco-Berber inscriptions date to around the 3rd century BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> the region was later divided into Mauretania Caesariensis and Mauretania Tingitana. Mauri from beyond the frontier crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-gibraltar">Strait of Gibraltar</a> to raid Baetica in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in the early 170s and again under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commodus">Commodus</a>, when they besieged Singilia Barba. Mauri cavalry served in Aurelian's army against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a> in 272, and units of Equites Mauri were later stationed along the Persian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> frontiers. Others resisted Rome: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximian">Maximian</a> campaigned against them for two years in 296–297, and in 372 the chieftain Firmus revolted. His brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gildo">Gildo</a> helped Theodosius the Elder defeat him, rose to command in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and rebelled in turn in 397.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> the Mauri took much of the interior and all the land west of Caesarea. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> conquered the Vandal kingdom in 533–534 they raided again, and in 544 the massacre of chiefs of the Levathi at a parley set off a rising in which the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> was killed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-troglita">John Troglita</a> defeated the revolt in 546–547, but further risings followed in 563 and 569 and under the Emperor Maurice.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Mauri resistance seems to have continued for about 50 years after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-conquest-of-the-maghreb">Muslim conquest of the Maghreb</a> ended Byzantine rule in the late 600s. The Chronicle of 754 still names the Mauri, but by the High Middle Ages the name had largely dropped out of Western documents, and Christian writers applied its descendant, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a>, to the Muslim populations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>. In 1903 French officials named the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> after the ancient kingdom, though it lies far to the south of it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mauri” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Mauri</b>, Berber peoples of northern Morocco, were united under kings by the late 3rd century BCE; the first well-known king, Baga, provided an escort to Masinissa in 206 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-mauretania">Kingdom of Mauretania</a></li></ul></section>
